UID:d612cac7-9e7e-4f7b-bae4-b88e6039ccf8@viewcy.olio
DTSTAMP:20260913T162421Z
DTSTART:20260913T190000Z
DTEND:20260913T213000Z
SUMMARY:The Stories We Inherit: A Four-Week Ancestry Workshop
DESCRIPTION:The Stories We Inherit: A Four-Week Family Mapping WorkshopMost
  of us have a sense that our family history matters. That the people who c
 ame before us\, and the dynamics between them\, live on in us in ways we f
 eel but can’t always understand. But few of us have had the chance to lo
 ok at the nuances of our ancestors' lives and their dynamics closely\, or 
 to approach our family members with genuine curiosity and ask the question
 s we've never thought to ask\, or were afraid to. Alongside family systems
  theory\, emerging research in fields like epigenetics has fueled curiosit
 y about the many ways the experiences of previous generations may continue
  to shape our lives today.Over four weeks\, we'll come together as a group
  and each build a genogram (visual map of our family) in order to deepen o
 ur understanding of where we come from and the patterns that have shaped u
 s. The genogram is grounded in family systems theory: the idea that a fami
 ly isn't a collection of separate individuals\, but an interconnected emot
 ional unit\, with a rich tapestry of emotional patterns.&nbsp\;Unlike a fa
 mily tree\, a genogram doesn't just track bloodlines. It captures the emot
 ional patterns between family members\, the texture of their relationships
 \, the influence of key events\, and the cultural and systemic forces that
  have shaped the family over time. As a group\, we’ll learn to read it\,
  and prepare for real conversations with family members in-between session
 s.We'll meet as a small group of no more than 15\, guided by Monica Setaru
 ddin and Kate Hakala — two MFT students who bring their personal practic
 e and clinical training to this work. As part of their training\, they con
 structed genograms and bore witness to each other’s stories. They discov
 ered how historical exploration can lead to personal transformation\, unlo
 cked curiosity\, and entirely new conversations with their oldest connecti
 ons. This is a tool typically used by MFT therapists and clients to facili
 tate relational healing. Seeing the impact it's had on us and the people i
 n our lives not just as clinicians but as humans\, we're eager to bring it
  outside the therapy room and into your hands.&nbsp\;This is a shared jour
 ney of guided self-discovery: a space to learn together through instructiv
 e sessions\, engage in self-inquiry\, and be in conversation with a small 
 community doing this work alongside you. While this is not therapy\, the f
 acilitators will approach your story with the positive regard\, reverence\
 , and openness it deserves.SessionsFour sessions\, 2.5 hours each\, Sunday
 s 3–5:30pm\, beginning September 13. A one-week break falls between sess
 ions 3 and 4 to give participants time to conduct their family interviews 
 before the group reconvenes.Over four weeks\, you will:Build a personal ge
 nogram spanning three generations of your familyLearn key concepts from fa
 mily systems theory in plain\, non-clinical languageIdentify recurring the
 mes and patterns running through your familyPrepare for and conduct real i
 nterviews with family member(s)Connect your family story to your present-d
 ay relationships with yourself and othersWitness and be witnessed in a sma
 ll\, trust-based communityWhat You'll ReceiveGuided teaching and discussio
 n: Weekly instruction grounded in family systems theory and relational the
 rapy\, made accessible in a non-clinical setting.Family map support: Guida
 nce in building your own genogram across three generations\, with facilita
 tor support along the way.Small-group learning community: A cohort of no m
 ore than 15 people\, building relationships and engaging in cross-dialogue
  over four weeks.Family interview preparation: Tools for initiating and co
 nducting conversations with family members — how to prepare\, how to han
 dle what surfaces\, and how to make meaning from what you hear.One-on-one 
 mentorship: Each participant receives a private conversation with the faci
 litators to discuss their family map\, themes\, and questions.Weekly take-
 home exercises: Reflection prompts and exercises to build your family map\
 , prepare for interviews\, and deepen personal inquiry between sessions.Ab
 out the TeachersThis series is facilitated by Monica Setaruddin and Kate H
 akala\, Marriage and Family Therapists in training and rising second-year 
 students in Iona University's MFT program.Monica SetaruddinMonica is deepl
 y passionate about family and relational therapy — particularly generati
 onal patterns\, cultural heritage\, and family lineage as pathways to self
 -awareness and insight into how we relate to ourselves and others. Her app
 roach weaves together training in family therapy\, years of personal work 
 in therapy\, Internal Family Systems\, and somatic and contemplative pract
 ices\, and twelve years working in global public health understanding how 
 systemic power and structural inequity shape families and communities.Kate
  HakalaKate was drawn to the field of relational therapy\, guided by a bel
 ief that healing happens within context. Her own generational experiences 
 of family blending and non-traditional caregiving have widened her definit
 ion of family and fostered her interest in group dynamics. For 15 years\, 
 she's worked as a writer and editor\, with a focus on connections\, cultur
 e\, and the issues facing modern relationships. By connecting individuals 
 to authenticity\, accountability\, and self-compassion\, she is invested i
 n helping others reauthor their lives.Who This Is ForThis series is open t
 o anyone curious about better understanding their families and the pattern
 s running through them. No background in therapy or family systems require
 d. You don't need to have a "difficult" family story — just a genuine in
 terest in understanding your own. This is best for people who have the tim
 e and capacity to engage between sessions. There will be homework: buildin
 g your family map\, reflecting on what you find\, and reaching out to fami
 ly members for conversations. The work that happens outside the room will 
 help guide our sessions each week.&nbsp\;At least one interview with a fam
 ily member is required for participation.ScholarshipsWe have a small numbe
 r of scholarship spots available for those facing financial barriers. If c
 ost is preventing you from participating\, please reach out with a short n
 ote about why this series would be meaningful for you. Scholarships are of
 fered on a rolling\, first-come basis.We especially welcome BIPOC individu
 als and children of immigrants\, recognizing that spaces centering these f
 amily experiences and histories can be rare.\n\nTickets & info: https://ww
 w.viewcy.com/event/the_stories_we_inheri
URL:https://www.viewcy.com/event/the_stories_we_inheri
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:14d0485a-b231-43c2-8400-7e21cc67ef00@viewcy.olio
DTSTAMP:20260913T162421Z
DTSTART:20260914T230000Z
DTEND:20260915T010000Z
SUMMARY:Myth\, Play\, and Shadow Work: A 4-Part Creativity Seminar
DESCRIPTION:In this four-week Olio\, we'll draw from Jungian psychology\, m
 ythology\, creativity studies\, and contemplative practice to get curious 
 about the critical voices that accompany creative life. Led by Justine Wil
 liams\, we'll gather an intimate group to spend four weeks transforming ou
 r relationship with creativity and with the inner critic that so often sha
 pes it.Rather than trying to conquer or silence these difficult parts\, we
 'll explore how to understand them\, engage with them\, and\, at times\, e
 ven collaborate with them. Along the way\, we may discover that the very c
 apacities we cultivate through creative practice—curiosity\, imagination
 \, play\, and compassion—are also the tools that allow us to meet these 
 voices differently. What once felt like an obstacle may become a source of
  insight\, depth\, and unexpected creative momentum.Wherever you are in yo
 ur creative journey\, for many people — and especially for artists — t
 he moment they begin to clarify a creative aspiration or imagine a differe
 nt future for themselves\, another voice quickly takes center stage: the i
 nner critic.Perhaps you've heard this voice:"I'm too lazy." "I'm not talen
 ted enough." "I'm too old to start." "I'll never\, I can't\, I won't..."Wh
 en these critical voices arise (which is a completely normal part of the c
 reative process)\, our instinct is often to fight them\, silence them\, or
  surrender to them altogether. A quick survey of popular self-help and inn
 er critic workshops suggests that this is the standard response: tame the 
 inner critic\, overcome the inner critic\, banish it\, erase it.In this fo
 ur-session Olio\, we'll take a different approach.We'll broaden our perspe
 ctive beyond the inner critic to explore the larger chorus of voices withi
 n us — some quieter\, yet supportive and wise. How might we recognize an
 d enlist these inner allies as we pursue creative work\, set meaningful go
 als\, and build a rich and fulfilling creative life?Through playful explor
 ation — not therapy\, and not self-improvement so much as a kind of crea
 tive detective work — we'll investigate this inner chorus and develop a 
 more dynamic relationship with our inner world. Participants will leave wi
 th greater clarity\, renewed creative momentum\, and practical tools for m
 oving more confidently toward a creative calling.Sessions Four sessions\, 
 Mondays 7:00–9:00pm\, beginning September 14 at the Olio Lighthouse.Sess
 ion One: The Mythology of the Inner Demon Learn the origins and archetypal
  dimensions of the inner critic through mythology\, folklore\, and Jungian
  psychology\, and begin to explore the personal mythologies\, creative bel
 ief systems\, and stories we carry.Session Two: Collaborating with Your In
 ner Critics Discover tools and approaches for understanding\, engaging\, a
 nd working constructively with critical inner voices.Session Three: Discov
 ering Inner Allies (and Other Voices) Identify supportive inner figures\, 
 values\, and perspectives that can help guide creative work and decision-m
 aking.Session Four: Creative Visioning Bring these insights together to cl
 arify a direction and envision the next chapter of your creative life.What
  to ExpectA grounded introduction to Jungian archetypes and shadow workFra
 meworks drawn from folklore\, mythology\, creativity studies\, and coachin
 gGuided exercises and creative practices designed to make the work persona
 l and accessibleAn approach to visioning and goal-setting that recognizes 
 how identity\, social location\, life circumstances\, and larger cultural 
 contexts shape what feels/is possible and meaningful for each of usA playf
 ul\, exploratory atmosphereOpportunities for reflection and conversation w
 ithin a small groupWho this is for&nbsp\;Do you sense that something creat
 ive is trying to emerge in your life? Perhaps there's a project you've bee
 n imagining\, a practice you'd like to begin\, a change you're considering
 \, or simply a desire to bring more creative purpose and expression into y
 our life? Are you an artist already pursuing a creative career\, but feel 
 passive or reactive\, and long to direct your creative life more powerfull
 y?Our Teacher: Justine Williams (she/they) Described as "wickedly funny" (
 SF Gate)\, Justine Williams is an artist\, performer\, filmmaker\, and edu
 cator whose work centers play\, collaboration\, and social imagination. Si
 nce 2013\, Justine has been on faculty at the Yale School of Drama\, where
  they teach acting through play and guide interdisciplinary collaboration.
 A celebrated teacher\, Justine has led workshops internationally and runs 
 The Play Society\, a roving school and creative lab dedicated to training 
 and new work development through an ethos of play and collaboration. They 
 hold an MFA in Performance and Interactive Media Arts from CUNY/Brooklyn C
 ollege.Justine's collaborations include a decade-long creative partnership
  with Eva Peskin exploring queer friendship and world-building\, and an on
 going project with artist and death doula Kate Muehlmann that invites publ
 ic engagement with questions of death and dying.Their work has been presen
 ted and supported by The Public Theater\, Ars Nova\, The Metropolitan Muse
 um of Art\, Abrons Arts Center\, Dixon Place\, New Georges\, Queer|Art\, T
 he Orchard Project\, and more.\n\nTickets & info: https://www.viewcy.com/e
 vent/calling_up_the_inner
URL:https://www.viewcy.com/event/calling_up_the_inner
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:9fefd3ac-8b93-48ae-90cb-846ef9ea3a1e@viewcy.olio
DTSTAMP:20260913T162421Z
DTSTART:20260918T000000Z
DTEND:20260918T013000Z
SUMMARY:Weddings: How Ceremonies Tell The Story of Civilization
DESCRIPTION:Every wedding tells a story—but not just about two people.It
 ’s a story about family. About religion. About power. About money. About
  hope. About what a culture believes love is supposed to look like.In this
  globe-spanning conversation\, we’ll wander through ancient rituals\, mo
 dern romance\, arranged marriages\, dating apps\, extravagant celebrations
 \, quiet ceremonies\, and the endlessly fascinating ways humans have tried
  to answer one impossible question: How do we build a life?Along the way w
 e’ll explore what weddings reveal about the societies that created them\
 , how our ideas of love have changed over time\, why relationships seem mo
 re complicated than ever\, and what all of this might say about where we
 ’re headed next.This talk will be part geography\, part history\, part p
 hilosophy and filled with stories from around the world that made us who w
 e are today.Anup Desai has been an adjunct professor of Geography at The C
 ollege of Staten Island for 15 years\, he is the founder of Rentah.com the
  Sharing Economy application\, and a wedding officiant that has presided o
 ver 30+ weddings across all faiths and sexualities. So if you want to get 
 married this night he can make it happen!Evening Flow7 PM – Doors open. 
 Settle in\, grab a drink\, and meet a friend.8 PM – The Olio begins: an 
 intimate\, interactive lecture and guided exploration.9 PM – We shift in
 to a social experience\, discussing ideas with others in the room while th
 e teacher joins and floats between conversations.10 PM - Last call at the 
 bar and we'll start to wind down the evening.What Is an Olio?Think Olio is
  an ongoing experiment in lifelong learning. We are a community of open-mi
 nded people who gather to exchange ideas with new friends and connect over
  art\, literature\, and philosophy.Check out our full calendarOur $30 memb
 ership gives you two events for the price of one!&nbsp\;An Olio is what we
  call the social learning events we’ve been organizing since 2015. A ble
 nd between a lecture and a house party\, Olios are led by a teacher who lo
 ves sharing their passions and the room is filled with curious\, open-mind
 ed New Yorkers.&nbsp\;Our goal is to spark new perspectives\, ignite curio
 sity\, and enjoy ourselves socially—an alternative to typical nightlife 
 options.Disclaimer: By purchasing a ticket\, you agree to have your photo/
 video used for social media/marketing materials. If you prefer to not have
  your photo taken\, feel free to send us a note.Photo by Zhi-Da Zhong\n\nT
 ickets & info: https://www.viewcy.com/event/geography_of_weddings
URL:https://www.viewcy.com/event/geography_of_weddings
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:82b0c9c8-fb7b-4a4f-8eec-4d8da9c08063@viewcy.olio
