SUMMARY:Listening Through Breath: A Guided Flute Concert
DESCRIPTION:Join us for an Olio with flutist and Juilliard educator Annie W
 u\, whose work sits at the intersection of classical performance\, mindful
  listening\, and the science of breath.Developed in collaboration with res
 earchers at Yale and Juilliard\, this Olio weaves six works for solo flute
  together with guided breathing exercises and embodied listening prompts. 
 The flute\, more than almost any instrument\, makes breath visible: every 
 phrase is an exhale\, every silence an inhale. To listen closely is to bre
 athe along.The evening will be a guided experience where the act of listen
 ing becomes its own form of regulation and restoration. Through the breath
 \, stories\, and techniques of a flutist\, this Olio asks what it might me
 an to truly take in music with our full attention. The concert will be fol
 lowed by a lightly guided conversation about the experience and a chance t
 o keep the conversation going with the others in the room.Evening Flow7 PM
  – Doors open. Settle in\, grab a drink\, and meet a friend.7:30 PM – 
 The Olio begins: an intimate\, interactive lecture and guided exploration.
 9 PM – We shift into a social experience\, discussing ideas with others 
 in the room while the teacher joins and floats between conversations.10 PM
  - Last call at the bar and we'll start to wind down the evening.What Is a
 n Olio?Think Olio is an ongoing experiment in lifelong learning. We are a 
 community of open-minded people who gather to exchange ideas with new frie
 nds and connect over art\, literature\, and philosophy.Check out our full 
 calendarOur $30 membership gives you two events for the price of one!&nbsp
 \;An Olio is what we call the social learning events we’ve been organizi
 ng since 2015. A blend between a lecture and a house party\, Olios are led
  by a teacher who loves sharing their passions and the room is filled with
  curious\, open-minded New Yorkers.&nbsp\;Our goal is to spark new perspec
 tives\, ignite curiosity\, and enjoy ourselves socially—an alternative t
 o typical nightlife options.Disclaimer: By purchasing a ticket\, you agree
  to have your photo/video used for social media/marketing materials. If yo
 u prefer to not have your photo taken\, feel free to send us a note.Photo 
 by Zhi-Da Zhong\n\nTickets & info: https://www.viewcy.com/event/embodied_l
 istening_f
URL:https://www.viewcy.com/event/embodied_listening_f
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e9cb6153-df46-43ba-8091-5489196d1093@viewcy.olio
DTSTAMP:20260913T162421Z
DTSTART:20260929T233000Z
DTEND:20260930T010000Z
SUMMARY:Olio Read-in: Dostoevsky's Notes From the Underground
DESCRIPTION:Put simply by one of our Olio members: "Read-Ins are like the b
 ook club that I actually want to be a part of. It's so much more fun to en
 gage with these texts as a group and with a teacher than as a solo activit
 y." “I am a sick man... I am a spiteful man. I think my liver is disease
 d.”So begins the maddening\, bitter and contradictory monologue of Dosto
 evsky’s hero in Notes from Underground. The Underground Man has no name\
 , and he enjoys his anonymity as he attacks virtually every aspect of huma
 n existence — laws\, nature\, mathematics\, science\, pursuit of happine
 ss and love.&nbsp\;In this Olio read-in\, Dina Odnopozova (PhD Yale\, prof
 essor at Baruch)&nbsp\; invites you into the Underground Man’s world thr
 ough the first chapter of Notes from Underground. Together\, we’ll encou
 nter one of literature’s most hyper-conscious—and profoundly irritatin
 g—characters in a conversation as intimate and unpredictable as a late-n
 ight argument at the bar. You may find yourself wanting to throw the Under
 ground Man out the window. Or perhaps sit him down\, give him a hug\, and 
 listen a little longer.Olio Read-ins were started by historian Jamie Warre
 n:"Rooted in the social revolution of the 1960s\, Read-ins have always bee
 n essential to the political project of building communities\, and bolster
 ing progressive change. Read-ins announce: We will not remain alone\, afra
 id\, and cut off from one another. No. We will sit side by side and with t
 he support of each other\, we will read. We will learn. We will enrich our
  inner world so that we can find the courage to exist outside of it."Teach
 er: Dina Odnopozova holds a PhD from Yale and is a professor at Baruch Col
 lege\, where her work explores the intersection of literature\, psychology
 \, and the unconscious. She has led Olios on Freud\, Jung\, and the archet
 ypal grammar of fairy tales — the witches\, wolves\, and forbidden doors
  that map the human subconscious.Think Olio is an ongoing experiment in li
 felong learning. We are a community of open-minded people who gather to ex
 change ideas with new friends and connect over art\, literature\, and phil
 osophy. Check out our full calendar Our $30 membership gives you two event
 s for the price of one!Our goal is to spark new perspectives\, ignite curi
 osity\, and enjoy ourselves socially—an alternative to typical nightlife
  options.Disclaimer: By purchasing a ticket\, you agree to have your photo
 /video used for social media/marketing materials. If you prefer to not hav
 e your photo taken\, feel free to send us a note. Photo by Zhi-Da Zhong\n\
 nTickets & info: https://www.viewcy.com/event/olio_readin_dos
URL:https://www.viewcy.com/event/olio_readin_dos
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:c36fcd16-ab17-4d1a-aa4a-3402b4ab1ca4@viewcy.olio
DTSTAMP:20260913T162421Z
DTSTART:20261001T230000Z
DTEND:20261002T010000Z
SUMMARY:Musical Ecology: A Study of Awareness
DESCRIPTION:Led by multi-instrumentalist and facilitator\, Joel Karabo Elli
 ott\, this Olio will explore the ways in which music interacts with the na
 tural environment\, and how to use this relationship to create more harmon
 y and vibrancy in your life and relationships.&nbsp\;Musical Ecology is a 
 study of awareness\, bringing together the textures of our landscape into 
 the vibrations of sound and music.&nbsp\; It is also a way of life\, to br
 ing us deeper into harmony with our surroundings\, with our fellow humans\
 , and within ourselves.&nbsp\;With a mix of musical performance\, lecture 
 and discussion - we will explore questions such as— what happens when we
  truly listen to the world around us?&nbsp\; What happens when we allow ou
 r own song and life to emerge from that listening?&nbsp\; How do we bring 
 harmony to a chaotic and disconnected world? And\, How do you live loud en
 ough for the world to hear\, and quiet enough to hear the world?About our 
 teacher for the evening: JoEl is a musical composer\, performer\, arts edu
 cator and community regeneration guide based in southern Appalachia\, USA 
 (Asheville\, North Carolina) and southern Africa (South Africa and Mozambi
 que). As the founder of Roots Grown Deep world music &amp\; healing arts e
 nsemble\, he has produced and released 12 albums of music and presented in
  17 countries around the world\, serving children\, adults\, communities a
 nd professional institutions with practical tools for the expansion of per
 sonal and collective enlightenment. He holds an M.A. in Consciousness &amp
 \; Human Potential from Maharishi Int’l University.More About Musical Ec
 ologyIn relation to human behavior and individual and collective conscious
 ness\, Musical Ecology is a reality-based way of Being informed by the sub
 jective and objective truth that all relative matter vibrates and creates 
 a sound\, and this sound has its source in eternal\, unchanged Consciousne
 ss. Thus\, Musical Ecology unites the biological with the ethereal\, the m
 aterial with the spiritual\, the organic with the celestial. As Maharishi 
 Mahesh Yogi says\, “All life hums with the organizing power of Natural L
 aw.”Evening Flow7 PM – Doors open. Settle in\, grab a drink\, and meet
  a friend.8 PM – The Olio begins: an intimate\, interactive lecture and 
 guided exploration.9 PM – We shift into a social experience\, discussing
  ideas with others in the room while the teacher joins and floats between 
 conversations.10 PM - Last call at the bar and we'll start to wind down th
 e evening.What Is an Olio?Think Olio is an ongoing experiment in lifelong 
 learning. We are a community of open-minded people who gather to exchange 
 ideas with new friends and connect over art\, literature\, and philosophy.
 Check out our full calendarOur $30 membership gives you two events for the
  price of one!&nbsp\;An Olio is what we call the social learning events we
 ’ve been organizing since 2015. A blend between a lecture and a house pa
 rty\, Olios are led by a teacher who loves sharing their passions and the 
 room is filled with curious\, open-minded New Yorkers.&nbsp\;Our goal is t
 o spark new perspectives\, ignite curiosity\, and enjoy ourselves socially
 —an alternative to typical nightlife options.Disclaimer: By purchasing a
  ticket\, you agree to have your photo/video used for social media/marketi
 ng materials. If you prefer to not have your photo taken\, feel free to se
 nd us a note.Photo by Zhi-Da Zhong\n\nTickets & info: https://www.viewcy.c
 om/event/musical_ecology_a_st
URL:https://www.viewcy.com/event/musical_ecology_a_st
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:77ea2d67-f7f9-430c-bdd4-5d186bcd106e@viewcy.olio
DTSTAMP:20260913T162421Z
DTSTART:20261006T230000Z
DTEND:20261007T010000Z
SUMMARY:Horny Gods\, Lying Heroes: Four Weeks Inside The Odyssey
DESCRIPTION:Even before the first trailers dropped\, the movie became an ap
 ple of discord that stirred polarizing debates about race\, identity\, gen
 der\, otherness\, appropriation\, ownerships and possession. This media Tr
 ojan war touches upon the very questions raised by the original epic: What
  does it mean to be away from home? What is home? What makes one an outsid
 er? How does one treat a stranger according to the laws of hospitality? Ho
 w does one treat a stranger if he is a “barbarian”? Or a monster? What
  is a monster? And what's up with all the terrifying female monsters that 
 haunt Odysseus?&nbsp\;Homer frequently called Odysseus polymetis—a word 
 that has troubled translators for centuries. It has been rendered as "inge
 nious\," "multitalented\," "crafty-minded\," and "skilled in all ways of c
 ontriving.” Looking past the heroic status Odysseus has accumulated over
  millennia\, the most accurate modern translation of this epithet might ju
 st be a "lying asshole." While his faithful wife Penelope weaves and unwea
 ves a shroud to delay remarriage\, Odysseus weaves endless stories out of 
 lies and tricks. He spends his journey cheating on his wife\, breaking and
  entering\, stealing cheese from mythical beasts\, gaslighting his crew un
 til they all die\, and acting like a privileged brat all along. So\, what 
 makes him one of the most popular heroes of all time? Why does his story s
 till capture our cultural imagination\, as evidenced by the massive craze 
 surrounding Christopher Nolan’s adaptation?In this four-part Olio\, Dina
  Odnopozova (PhD Yale\, professor at Baruch)&nbsp\; will approach the Odys
 sey as a vast encyclopedia of ancient knowledge. We will delve in its soci
 al mores\, traditions\, debates\, prejudices\, laws\, etymologies\, meals\
 , rituals\, maps and horny gods. While the epic describes a distant cultur
 e\, it also contains a repository of psychological archetypes that we enco
 unter in our nightmares and individual journeys: apples of discord that pr
 ey on our vanity\, cyclopes who threaten us with sheer otherness\, green h
 erbs and beautiful strangers that make us forget the way home.&nbsp\;Sessi
 onsFour sessions\, Tuesdays 7pm-9pm\, starting October 6\, 13\, 20\, 27 at
  the Olio Lighthouse.Each class focuses on a single section of The Odyssey
 . You are encouraged to read each section prior to class.Telemachy (Books 
 I–IV)\, Wanderings (Books V–XII)\, Homecoming (Books XIII–XIX) and V
 engeance (Books XX–XXIV).\n\nTickets & info: https://www.viewcy.com/even
 t/odyssey
URL:https://www.viewcy.com/event/odyssey
END:VEVENT
